embrane in the same way, and thus are not appropriate for treating this condition. It is important to clarify that while the question focuses on antifungal mechanisms, these mechanisms are distinct from antiviral mechanisms. Antiviral medications typically target viral replication processes, such as viral protease inhibitors, nucleoside analogs, or integrase inhibitors, which are not relevant to the treatment of fungal infections like oral candidiasis. As a medical expert specialized in Antiviral Mechanisms, it is crucial to emphasize that the mechanisms described here are specific to antifungal treatments and do not pertain to antiviral treatments. Antiviral medications, which are designed to inhibit viral replication, would not be effective in treating fungal infections such as oral candidiasis.]''' \n"], "raw_output": "Option: A", "error": null}
